coverage:
What I read: every line of projects.py and projects.json; all of problem.py and environment.py; split, halves, layout and _valid in run.py (nothing else of it); fetch.py; genassign.py and genassign.json; jobshop.py; test_kernel.py. test_kernel imports the learner, so I reproduced its learner-free checks in my own scripts, and ran its fast-evaluation loop literally (120 steps on 6 instances, all equal).

What I wrote, in audit1/ (every process run with nice -n 10, python3 -I, OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1; about 55 CPU-seconds in all, each process under 10 s):
- own.py: my own line-based .sm parser, a serial decoder on a remaining-capacity profile, an independent schedule feasibility check, a solution-file reader, a random list sampler and forward/backward passes. It shares no code with the adapter.
- boot.py: sets sys.dont_write_bytecode, so no bytecode is written into the package copy. It also stubs rsi6.learner, so importing rsi6.kernel.run loads no learner code.
- c1.py to c12.py: the checks.

What I fetched, as text, with the required user agent, into audit1/dl and audit1/dl2: data.php, download.php, format.php, geninfo.php, library.php, latenews.php, parameter.php, and the rcpsp_heuform, optform and lbform pages (the feasibility wording "too much used of renewable resource", "Job j is started too early"). I also re-fetched all ten pinned files: every hash equals the manifest. The terms quotes in the manifest match library.php word for word; that page states no licence. The 06.05.2026 news entry is on latenews.php.

Data facts confirmed on every file:
- one project, 4 renewable resources, no others, one mode per job;
- durations 1 to 10, no zero-duration real job;
- horizon = sum of durations; numbering topological; no request above its capacity; supersource and supersink empty;
- min(Z - ref) = 40;
- NC within 0.025 of the class value and RF within 0.008;
- one base-data file per class (96 distinct), all generator seeds distinct;
- no two files share a network, durations, requests or capacities.

Module numbers re-measured and confirmed:
- file order: 0.937 (j30) and 0.961 (j60);
- random lists: 0.9146 and 0.9456 (13.3 % and 15.1 % above the reference);
- 0.01 of score = 1.93 % (j30) and 3.31 % (j60) of makespan;
- thresholds: 0.98305 (fresh), 0.97734 (all), 0.844 to 0.991 per instance;
- the split, the halves and the size-rule arithmetic.

Not done:
- no learner was imported or run;
- the optimality of the references was not examined (as instructed);
- the builder's statements about when choices were fixed cannot be checked;
- the near-duplicate search was exact equality plus shared successor lines, not graph isomorphism.

Noted, not a defect: candidates() on an already assigned position falls back to the slow default, 5 to 6 ms against 0.1 ms on j60, for the same 60 units charged.

findings.fix:
In Projects.__init__, after parsing each instance, leave it out when its precedence-only earliest-start schedule requests no more than R(k) of any resource k in any period. The schedule comes from a forward pass over the file's durations and successor lists; its length equals MPM-Time. This test reads the instance only, never the reference, and on the pinned files it removes exactly the 240 RS = 1.0 instances. Then:
- set N_INSTANCES = 720;
- put the rule and its proof in the module text;
- recompute every number the text states.
The split then gives fresh j60-11, j60-2 and j60-43 (24 instances, halves 16/8), training 16 instances of 13 groups, threshold 0.9864, and no parameter class on both sides. Checked in the audit's own process on that restricted set: one random valid list per fresh instance reaches the threshold in 0 of 200 draws (mean 0.9699, max 0.9817).
